<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lament · 1969 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Alfonsina y el mar</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A quiet Argentine song from 1969 written in memory of the poet Alfonsina Storni.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Félix Luna</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Ariel Ramírez</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Argentine folk music</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Argentina</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>first released 1969, on Mercedes Sosa's album Mujeres argentinas</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words by Félix Luna, music by Ariel Ramírez (first released 1969))</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonsina-y-el-mar">Alfonsina y el mar</a>&quot; (&quot;Alfonsina and the sea&quot;) is a zamba with music by the Argentine pianist Ariel Ramírez and words by the Argentine writer Félix Luna. It was first released in 1969 on the singer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercedes-sosa">Mercedes Sosa</a>'s album Mujeres argentinas.</p><p>The song is a tribute to the Argentine poet Alfonsina Storni, who took her own life in 1938, going into the sea from a jetty. It became a classic, and singers of many nationalities have recorded it since.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A tribute and lament for the poet Alfonsina Storni and her death in the sea. Its mood is quiet, tender and mournful.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so the Institute does not print them itself: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="262893" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/Mercedes-sosa-alfonsina-y-el-mar-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
